# /etc/udev/rules.d/99-fpms-npu.rules
#
# Device access to the RK3588S NPU for the unprivileged `ubuntu` user.
#
# THE PROBLEM THIS SOLVES
# =======================
# SPEC.md names this file. It was never written. Nothing in the image grants
# `ubuntu` access to the NPU device node - and EVERY FPMS service runs as
# `ubuntu`, including fpms-rover-agent and fpms-npud, both of which reach the
# NPU through /usr/lib/librknnrt.so.
#
# An EACCES on the device node is invisible at every level an operator checks:
#
#   - RKNNLite.init_runtime() RETURNS an int. It does not raise. bench_npu.py
#     does `r.load_rknn(y.MODEL); r.init_runtime()` and checks neither return
#     value, which is exactly how a permission failure becomes a silent one.
#   - fpms_rover_agent.py wraps the whole NPU block - import, load_rknn,
#     init_runtime, labels - in ONE try/except that logs
#         "NPU unavailable (...); streaming without detection"
#     once, and then carries on forever.
#
# So the symptom of "the rules file was never written" is: every unit active,
# camera streaming, dashboard alive, LiDAR fine, and ZERO detections, for as
# long as the rover runs. NPU_SPEC.md §0 calls this the single worst failure
# mode in the project. This file is one of the two things that prevent it (the
# other is agent 7's selftest, which reports it).
#
#
# WHICH GROUP, AND WHY `video` AND NOT A DEDICATED `npu` GROUP
# ============================================================
# Decision: GROUP="video". A dedicated `npu` group was considered and rejected.
#
#   1. `video` already exists on Ubuntu 22.04 and the ubuntu-rockchip base
#      already puts `ubuntu` in it (it is how the camera works today).
#      VERIFY ON HARDWARE:  id ubuntu | tr ',' '\n' | grep -E 'video|render'
#   2. 99-fpms-serial.rules already binds the camera with GROUP="video". One
#      convention for "hardware the rover's one unprivileged user may open" is
#      easier to audit than two.
#   3. A dedicated group is a SILENT-FAILURE MACHINE. It needs two things that
#      live in two different agents' files: the group must be created by a
#      build stage, and `ubuntu` must be added to it. Miss either half and
#      udev cannot resolve the name, logs "unknown group" into a journal
#      nobody reads, and leaves the node root:root 0600 - which is byte for
#      byte the situation this file exists to fix, with a file present that
#      looks like it fixed it.
#   4. Group membership is read at process start. A group created on FIRST
#      BOOT, after the services are already running, does not reach them until
#      a restart. `video` is in the passwd database before anything starts.
#
# The cost is honest: `video` is broader than the NPU. On a single-purpose
# rover with one unprivileged user that must already reach the camera, that is
# an acceptable price for a rule that cannot half-apply.
#
#
# ############################################################################
# # !! VERIFY ON HARDWARE - THE NODE NAME IS NOT CONFIRMED !!                #
# #                                                                          #
# # The board has not arrived. NOTHING below has run on an RK3588S.          #
# #                                                                          #
# # Rockchip's BSP rknpu driver exposes the NPU in more than one way          #
# # depending on kernel config: as a misc character device (/dev/rknpu), or   #
# # as a DRM device (/dev/dri/card* + /dev/dri/renderD*) when the driver is   #
# # built with its DRM GEM allocator. Both configurations exist in the wild   #
# # and this image does not build the kernel, so which one ships is a         #
# # property of the base image, not of FPMS-OS. The rules below cover both    #
# # and are written so that the ones that do not match are inert.            #
# #                                                                          #
# # Confirm, in this order, on the running board:                             #
# #                                                                          #
# #     ls -l /dev/rknpu* /dev/dri/ /dev/dma_heap/ 2>&1                       #
# #     sudo dmesg | grep -i rknpu                                            #
# #     udevadm info -a -n /dev/dri/renderD129 | grep -m6 -E 'KERNEL|SUBSYSTEM|DRIVERS'
# #     udevadm info -a -n /dev/rknpu | grep -m6 -E 'KERNEL|SUBSYSTEM|DRIVERS'
# #                                                                          #
# # Then confirm WHICH node librknnrt actually opens - do not infer it:      #
# #                                                                          #
# #     sudo apt install -y strace                                            #
# #     strace -f -e trace=openat python3 -c \                                #
# #       "from rknnlite.api import RKNNLite; r=RKNNLite(); \                 #
# #        r.load_rknn('/home/ubuntu/yolo/yolo26n-rk3588.rknn'); \            #
# #        print('init_runtime ->', r.init_runtime())" 2>&1 | grep -E 'dri|rknpu|dma_heap'
# #                                                                          #
# # `init_runtime -> 0` is success. ANY other number is a failure that the    #
# # agent would have swallowed.                                              #
# #                                                                          #
# # After editing this file:  sudo udevadm control --reload                   #
# #                           sudo udevadm trigger                            #
# #                                                                          #
# # fpms-selftest reports a FAIL if `ubuntu` cannot init the runtime, so a    #
# # wrong guess here cannot pass silently - but it is a five minute job to    #
# # get right up front.                                                      #
# ############################################################################
#
#
# NOTE ON SCOPE: the /dev/fpms-npu symlink below is DIAGNOSTIC CONVENIENCE for
# the selftest and for an operator at a prompt. librknnrt.so opens the real
# node by its own hardcoded path and never consults a symlink, so renaming or
# removing the symlink changes nothing about whether inference works. The
# GROUP=/MODE= are the load-bearing part.

# --- Case A: rknpu as a misc / plain character device -----------------------
#
# The classic layout, and the one NPU_SPEC.md's shared contract names
# (/dev/rknpu). Default for a misc device is root:root 0600, so without this
# rule `ubuntu` gets EACCES.
#
# KERNEL=="rknpu*" rather than =="rknpu" because some BSP revisions register a
# second node alongside it for the memory allocator. Matching the family costs
# nothing; a node that does not exist matches nothing.
#
# No SUBSYSTEM== filter: whether this lands under `misc` or a bare `rknpu`
# class differs between BSP revisions, and pinning it to the wrong subsystem
# is the failure mode where the file exists and does nothing.
KERNEL=="rknpu*", GROUP="video", MODE="0660", SYMLINK+="fpms-npu"

# --- Case B: rknpu as a DRM RENDER node -------------------------------------
#
# When the driver is built with its DRM GEM allocator the NPU appears as a DRM
# device and userspace uses the RENDER node (renderD*), not the primary node.
#
# DRIVERS=="rknpu" is what keeps this off the Mali GPU, which is also a DRM
# device on this SoC and will also have a renderD* node. Getting that filter
# wrong would re-permission the GPU instead of the NPU.
# VERIFY: udevadm info -a -n /dev/dri/renderD129 | grep -m3 DRIVERS
#
# MODE="0666" GROUP="render" deliberately RESTATES systemd's own default for
# renderD* (50-udev-default.rules) instead of tightening it to 0660 video.
# Two reasons, both about not making things worse:
#   - 0666 already grants `ubuntu` everything it needs, so tightening buys
#     nothing on a single-user rover;
#   - if DRIVERS=="rknpu" turns out to match the GPU on this BSP, a tightened
#     rule would take Mali away from anything else that wants it, and that
#     failure would look nothing like an NPU problem.
# The value of this rule in the DRM case is therefore the SYMLINK and the fact
# that the file documents the case - not a permission change.
SUBSYSTEM=="drm", KERNEL=="renderD*", DRIVERS=="rknpu", \
  GROUP="render", MODE="0666", SYMLINK+="fpms-npu"

# --- Case C: rknpu DRM PRIMARY node -----------------------------------------
#
# Belt and braces. librknnrt is expected to use the render node (case B) - the
# render node exists precisely so unprivileged clients need no DRM auth - but
# that expectation is UNVERIFIED (see the strace command in the box above). If
# it turns out to want the primary node, this is the rule that saves the day.
# root:video 0660 is what a card* node should already be on a seatless
# headless box; stating it removes the dependency on that being true.
SUBSYSTEM=="drm", KERNEL=="card*", DRIVERS=="rknpu", \
  GROUP="video", MODE="0660"

# --- Case D: DMA-BUF heaps ---------------------------------------------------
#
# VERIFY ON HARDWARE - include or delete this rule based on the strace above.
#
# RKNPU2 2.x runtimes allocate their input/output buffers through DMA-BUF heaps
# (/dev/dma_heap/system, .../system-uncached) when they are available; the heap
# nodes default to root:root 0600, so an unprivileged process gets EACCES and
# the runtime silently falls back to a slower allocator - or fails, depending
# on version. Neither outcome announces itself.
#
# This is the one rule here that genuinely widens attack surface: `video`
# members can allocate kernel DMA memory. On a rover with one unprivileged
# user that already owns the camera and the NPU, that is not a meaningful
# escalation, but if the strace shows librknnrt never touches /dev/dma_heap,
# DELETE this rule rather than keep it "just in case".
SUBSYSTEM=="dma_heap", KERNEL=="system*", GROUP="video", MODE="0660"


# ############################################################################
# # DEBUGFS: /sys/kernel/debug/rknpu/ - UDEV CANNOT FIX THIS                 #
# ############################################################################
#
# Three consumers in this layer want to read debugfs as `ubuntu`:
#
#     /sys/kernel/debug/rknpu/version   driver version   (selftest, agent 7)
#     /sys/kernel/debug/rknpu/load      per-core load    (agents 4 and 7)
#
# They cannot, and no rule in this file can change that. debugfs entries are
# not devices, so udev never sees them - and systemd mounts /sys/kernel/debug
# itself as 0700 root:root, so even a world-readable file underneath is
# unreachable because `ubuntu` cannot traverse the mount point.
#
# The fix belongs to whoever owns the mount and the sudoers rules. In
# preference order:
#
#   1. MOUNT OPTIONS (preferred - narrow, declarative, no per-file chmod).
#      debugfs honours uid=/gid=/mode=. Mount it 0750 owned by the `video`
#      group, so members can traverse and nobody else can even list it:
#          debugfs /sys/kernel/debug debugfs defaults,mode=0750,gid=video 0 0
#      VERIFY ON HARDWARE:
#          sudo mount -o remount,mode=0750,gid=video /sys/kernel/debug
#          sudo -u ubuntu cat /sys/kernel/debug/rknpu/version
#
#   2. A NARROW SUDOERS RULE (agent E owns /etc/sudoers.d/). Note the warning
#      already in 50-overlay.sh: sudo matches the LITERAL argv, so the rule and
#      the call site must agree exactly or the escalation becomes a silent
#      password prompt.
#          ubuntu ALL=(root) NOPASSWD: /bin/cat /sys/kernel/debug/rknpu/version, \
#                                      /bin/cat /sys/kernel/debug/rknpu/load
#
#   3. NO PRIVILEGE AT ALL - and this is the one to reach for first. The
#      DRIVER version is also reported by the userspace runtime, without
#      debugfs and without root:
#          python3 -c "from rknnlite.api import RKNNLite; r=RKNNLite(); \
#            r.load_rknn('/home/ubuntu/yolo/yolo26n-rk3588.rknn'); \
#            r.init_runtime(); print(r.get_sdk_version())"
#      It prints an API line (the librknnrt version) and a DRV line (the
#      kernel driver version), which is exactly the pair
#      /etc/fpms/npu-versions.json needs checked.
#      VERIFY ON HARDWARE: confirm get_sdk_version() reports DRV on this
#      runtime version - it needs a loaded model and a successful
#      init_runtime(), so it cannot substitute for debugfs when the NPU is the
#      thing that is broken. Per-core `load` has no userspace equivalent.
#
# REJECTED: doing it from here with RUN+="/bin/chmod ...". It would have to
# loosen /sys/kernel/debug itself, exposing every other debugfs interface on
# the box to get at two files; it races the creation of the debugfs entries at
# module init; and udev RUN+= is the wrong place for filesystem policy. A
# comment that says "we chose not to" is worth more than a rule that half
# works.
